class.description:
[ResourceTextSerializer] maps the properties of a [Resource] that have [constant @GlobalScope.PROPERTY_USAGE_STORAGE] to text that players and other tools can read and edit. The same mapping is used by [ResourceSaver] and [ResourceLoader] for the file extensions [code].json[/code], [code].ini[/code], [code].csv[/code] and [code].xml[/code] (and [code].toml[/code] with the TOML module), so in most cases a call to [method ResourceSaver.save] and [method ResourceLoader.load] is all that is needed. [method serialize] and [method deserialize] do the same with strings, for example to send a resource over the network or to store it in a database. The format is a value of [enum Format]; [method get_available_formats] lists the formats of the current build.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var settings = AudioBusSettings.new() # A Resource with class_name AudioBusSettings.
settings.master_volume = 0.8
ResourceSaver.save(settings, "user://audio.json")
ResourceSaver.save(settings, "user://audio.xml")

var loaded = ResourceLoader.load("user://audio.json") as AudioBusSettings
print(loaded.master_volume) # Prints 0.8

# The same as text, without a file:
var text = ResourceTextSerializer.serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_XML)
var copy = ResourceTextSerializer.deserialize(text, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_XML) as AudioBusSettings

# Options are a SerializerOptions resource (null = the project settings).
var options = SerializerOptions.new()
options.indent = ""
options.enums_as_names = false
var compact = ResourceTextSerializer.serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_JSON, options)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var settings = new AudioBusSettings(); // A [GlobalClass] Resource.
settings.MasterVolume = 0.8f;
ResourceSaver.Save(settings, "user://audio.json");
ResourceSaver.Save(settings, "user://audio.xml");

var loaded = ResourceLoader.Load<AudioBusSettings>("user://audio.json");
GD.Print(loaded.MasterVolume); // Prints 0.8

// The same as text, without a file:
string text = ResourceTextSerializer.Serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Xml);
var copy = (AudioBusSettings)ResourceTextSerializer.Deserialize(text, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Xml);

// Options are a SerializerOptions resource (null = the project settings).
var options = new SerializerOptions { Indent = "", EnumsAsNames = false };
string compact = ResourceTextSerializer.Serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Json, options);
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]JSON[/b] ([constant FORMAT_JSON]): one object per resource. The root object has the keys [code]"$type"[/code] (the class name, for example [code]"AudioBusSettings"[/code]) and [code]"$format_version"[/code] (see [constant TEXT_LAYOUT_VERSION]), followed by one key per property in property order. Nested resources are nested objects, arrays of resources are arrays of objects. A nested object only has a [code]"$type"[/code] key if its class differs from the declared type of the property. The type is looked up in the first 4 KB of a file only (savers write it first). A [code].json[/code] file without a [code]"$type"[/code] key that names a [Resource] class is still loaded as [JSON], as before (and [code].ini[/code], [code].xml[/code], [code].toml[/code] and [code].yaml[/code] files without one load as [INI], [XML], [TOML] and [YAML]); [method ResourceLoader.load] with a type hint (for example [code]load(path, "AudioBusSettings")[/code]) also reads such files as that type.
[b]INI[/b] ([constant FORMAT_INI]): one section per resource. The root resource is in [code][root][/code] with the key [code]$type[/code]; a nested resource is in its own section named after its path, for example [code][root/entries/0][/code]. By default the values use the Variant syntax of [ConfigFile] ([code]name="Ann"[/code], [code]size=Vector2(1, 2)[/code]). With [member SerializerOptions.ini_value_style] (or the project setting [member ProjectSettings.filesystem/resources/text_formats/ini_plain_values]), values are written without type syntax ([code]name=Ann[/code], [code]size=1, 2[/code], [code]color=#ff0000ff[/code]), so other tools can read them. Both styles are read.
[b]CSV[/b] ([constant FORMAT_CSV]): only for list resources: a resource with exactly one typed array of resources (for example [code]Array[Entry][/code]) whose elements have only simple properties (no resources, arrays or dictionaries). The file has a header row with the property names and one row per element (see [member SerializerOptions.csv_headers] and [member SerializerOptions.csv_delimiter]). A CSV file contains no type, so it must be loaded with a type hint: [code]ResourceLoader.load("user://voices.csv", "VoiceList")[/code] or [code]deserialize(text, FORMAT_CSV, &"VoiceList")[/code]; without a hint, it loads as a [CSV] resource. A resource that does not fit gives an error and no file is written; check it first with [method can_serialize].
[b]XML[/b] ([constant FORMAT_XML]): the root element [code]<resource type="AudioBusSettings" format_version="1">[/code] has one child element per property, in property order: [code]<master_volume>0.8</master_volume>[/code]. Integers, floats, booleans and strings are text; a float always has a [code].[/code] ([code]1.0[/code]). Nested resources and dictionaries are elements with one child element per key; a key that is not a valid XML name is written as [code]<entry key="my key">[/code]. Arrays have [code]kind="array"[/code]: arrays of numbers (vectors, packed arrays) as one text ([code]<offset kind="array">0.5, -2.0</offset>[/code]), other arrays with one [code]<item>[/code] per element. [code]kind="string"[/code] marks a string that would otherwise be read as a number or boolean ([code]<code kind="string">007</code>[/code]), [code]kind="null"[/code] marks [code]null[/code], [code]kind="dictionary"[/code] an empty dictionary. A nested resource has a [code]type[/code] attribute only if its class differs from the declared type. Comments and CDATA sections are allowed when editing by hand. A [code].xml[/code] file is only loaded as a resource if its root element is [code]<resource>[/code] with a [code]type[/code] attribute that names a [Resource] class; other XML files are left to other loaders.
[b]TOML[/b] ([constant FORMAT_TOML]): implemented by the TOML module (see [TOML]). [b]YAML[/b] ([constant FORMAT_YAML]): implemented by the YAML module (see [YAML]).
[b]Values:[/b] [Color] is written as [code]"#rrggbbaa"[/code] when that is exact, otherwise as an array of floats; vectors, rectangles and matrices as arrays of numbers; enums as their names (see [member SerializerOptions.enums_as_names]); [StringName] and [NodePath] as strings. A resource that is saved in its own file is written as its path and loaded from that path. When loading, a key that is missing keeps the default value of the class, and an unknown key is skipped with a warning. Values are converted to the type of the property, so a hand-edited [code]"0.5"[/code] or [code]"yes"[/code] is accepted for a [float] or [bool] property.
[b]Options:[/b] [method serialize], [method deserialize] and [method resource_to_dictionary] take a [SerializerOptions]. [code]null[/code] uses the project settings under [code]filesystem/resources/text_formats/[/code], which [ResourceSaver] and [ResourceLoader] use as well.
[b]Note:[/b] JSON has only one number type. Integers above 2^53 lose precision, and properties of type [Variant] get [float] values back where [int] values were saved. In plain INI and in CSV, properties of type [Variant] come back as [String]. XML and TOML keep [int], [float], [bool] and [String] apart.
[b]Note:[/b] [code].csv[/code] files in [code]res://[/code] are imported as translations by the editor. Keep CSV resources in [code]user://[/code], or set their importer to "Keep File" in the Import dock.
[b]Note:[/b] The methods of this class can be called from any thread.

method.description:
Creates a resource from [param text] in the text format [param format]. The class comes from the type stored in the text ([code]"$type"[/code] in JSON and TOML, [code]$type[/code] in the INI section [code][root][/code], the [code]type[/code] attribute of the XML root element), or from [param type_hint]. CSV text and text written with [member SerializerOptions.include_type_marker] set to [code]false[/code] have no type, so [param type_hint] is required there. Of [param options], CSV reads [member SerializerOptions.csv_delimiter] and [member SerializerOptions.csv_headers]; [code]null[/code] uses the project settings. Returns [code]null[/code] and prints an error if the text can't be read or the format is not available.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var options = SerializerOptions.new()
options.csv_delimiter = ";"
var list = ResourceTextSerializer.deserialize(csv_text, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_CSV, &"VoiceList", options)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var options = new SerializerOptions { CsvDelimiter = ";" };
var list = ResourceTextSerializer.Deserialize(csvText, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Csv, "VoiceList", options);
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]

Returns [param resource] as text in the format [param format]. The text is the same that [ResourceSaver] writes into a file with that extension when [param options] is [code]null[/code] (the project settings). See [SerializerOptions] for the options. Returns an empty string and prints an error if the format is not available (for example [constant FORMAT_TOML] without the TOML module) or the resource can't be written (for example if it contains itself, or does not fit CSV).
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var options = SerializerOptions.new()
options.indent = ""
options.enums_as_names = false
var compact = ResourceTextSerializer.serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_JSON, options)
var xml = ResourceTextSerializer.serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.FORMAT_XML)
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
var options = new SerializerOptions { Indent = "", EnumsAsNames = false };
string compact = ResourceTextSerializer.Serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Json, options);
string xml = ResourceTextSerializer.Serialize(settings, ResourceTextSerializer.Format.Xml);
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
